endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This chapter discusses alternatives for improving the New Point accounting system. Improving communication with suppliers is the main objective of this chapter. Three workable solutions are available, each designed to improve the company's operations and address its problems and constraints. We used the solution possibilities matrix and the weighted scoring technique to evaluate each option and identify the alternative. The chapter also explores the feasibility study for future assurance from a technical and operational perspective.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
445 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (3.2 Proposed systems)
/E (3.2 Proposed systems)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (3.2.1 Systems description)
/E (3.2.1 Systems description)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
447 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Solution 1: Automated Supplier Communication Feature)
/E (Solution 1: Automated Supplier Communication Feature)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
448 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The first solution seeks to integrate an automated supplier communication module into the New Point system. This feature automatically detects low inventory levels and sends notifications to registered suppliers who request quotations. Suppliers can respond via the system portal, and employees can compare them after receiving all quotations.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The solution provides a dashboard to allow employees to compare quotations and categorize suppliers based on prices and delivery times, automatically arranging them. By automating these two processes, the company saves time and reduces human error, taking the company to the next level.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Overall, this solution enhances operational efficiency, seeks to improve supplier relationships, and ensures better, timely decisions are made based on data.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (13)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Chapter 3: Alternative Analysis)
/E (Chapter 3: Alternative Analysis)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Solution 2: Separate database for inventory and purchasing management)
/E (Solution 2: Separate database for inventory and purchasing management)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
454 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (An independent database for purchasing and inventory management. This solution proposes creating an independent inventory and purchasing management system separate from the NewPoint system. This system manages supplier data, bids, and purchase orders, and allows for continuous tracking of inventory levels. Employees input supplier data, community categories, and reorder points, and the system then automatically generates a request for quotation \(FFQ\) and sends it to approved suppliers.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
455 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This solution tracks inventory in real time and provides accurate data on parts availability. The system can also be linked to the NewPoint system to synchronize financial data. The disadvantages of this solution are that it requires additional setup and higher maintenance costs, but it provides greater flexibility and the possibility of future expansion as the business grows.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Solution 3: Maintain the current system. )
/E (Solution 3: Maintain the current system. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The third solution proposes maintaining the system as is, continuing to use the same processes when communicating with suppliers, comparing bids, and managing purchase orders. The most significant advantages of this solution are its low cost and the lack of need for technical updates. However, it will not address any of the fundamental issues currently facing the organization, such as slow processes, decreased efficiency, and increased errors.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In the long run, this solution will lead to slower workflow, loss of competitiveness, and increased operating costs. Therefore, it is not recommended to pursue this solution because it does not offer any real improvement to the current system.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (14)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
